weave-knee trial v1
fanout: 3
seed: 2
weaver: claude-sonnet-5
judge: claude-sonnet-5
quality: 3
narrative:
  Dordor moves work through a cycle of three modules. Nexsax, the courier, hands its sealed ledger to Kelnex, but only once 2 of its peers countersign, and it answers any read from its newest snapshot only; when a handoff fails, Nexsax retries it at most 4 times before parking it. Kelnex, the scheduler, receives that sealed ledger and likewise acts only once 5 of its peers countersign, answering its own reads from its newest snapshot only; a failed handoff at Kelnex is retried at most 3 times before being parked. Kelnex then publishes its sweep verdicts to Toldor, the broker. Toldor refuses new writes while its own sweep is running, evicts anything left unclaimed after 6 ticks, and — like Kelnex — retries a failed handoff at most 3 times before parking it. Closing the loop, Toldor signals backpressure to Nexsax, so the courier that started the chain is the one throttled by the broker at its far end.

  The result is a closed circuit: Nexsax delivers to Kelnex, Kelnex reports its sweep verdicts to Toldor, and Toldor pushes backpressure onto Nexsax. Within that circuit, two of the three modules — Kelnex and Nexsax — gate their own action on peer countersignature (five peers for Kelnex, two for Nexsax) and guard every read by consulting only their newest snapshot, while Toldor instead gates itself by refusing writes mid-sweep and by evicting unclaimed items after 6 ticks. Two of the three — Kelnex and Toldor — share the same three-retry parking threshold, while Nexsax alone extends to four retries before parking a failed handoff.
